186193573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193574 #edb/ref 1061 40166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c4c30b3-081a-57b6-a2af-8ec7f827411f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193580 #edb/ref 1061 40259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "450ba46d-28c8-58be-945a-54fa0106cb15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193788 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193788 #edb/ref 1061 8694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193788 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17a99595-6232-50d3-939f-80b3dee5b912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193788 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193789 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193789 #edb/ref 1061 8695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193789 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81ac2285-c162-59f3-94c2-bfff181b2ee6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193789 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193812 #edb/ref 1064 "Two lines are **parallel** if they never meet. So, for example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are parallel.\n\n![](images/d9/d9bcd3cb4e201292afd4c3dca68273f16cff43d76c5a6a2f80348f5d8db1bd4c.png)\n\nIf two lines are parallel, we can express this using the $\\parallel$ symbol as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\parallel s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is parallel to the line $s$.\"\n\nIf two lines are not parallel, they must have a common point. In this case, we say that the lines **intersect**.\n\nFor example, the lines $v$ and $w$ below intersect at the point $P{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a659c4a23e60880656c4fea0ec8e6ca63e0d9e55704fdb53b5bdf1a6e30b89e3.png)\n\nWe have the following important property about lines in two-dimensional space.\n\n> *Any two distinct lines are either parallel or intersect at exactly one point.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193812 #edb/ref 1065 2162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193812 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b5d86f95-6437-58f6-a35f-cf72da79271b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193812 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193847 #edb/ref 1064 "Two lines are **perpendicular** if they intersect and form a $90^\\circ$ angle.\n\nFor example, the lines $r$ and $s$ below are perpendicular.\n\n![](images/73/73d2e836e942d6e468c8d9652921a9321b842d47b54ba59529123da61a9a96b4.png)\n\nWhen two lines are perpendicular, we can express this with the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\nr \\perp s\n$$\n\nIn words, we say, \"the line $r$ is perpendicular to the line $s$.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193847 #edb/ref 1065 6653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193847 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2580c600-ddf7-5d7e-983d-e971ce3c6700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193847 #edb/ref 1067 "Perpendicular Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
